---
.title = "The relay",
.description = "A relay carries a browser peer's keyed CapTP session over a WebSocket to a peer that has a socket. Only bytes cross it. It holds no capability of yours.",
.date = .date("2026-07-21T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

A browser cannot listen on or dial a raw TCP or QUIC connection. The relay is
a small WebSocket service that carries a browser's
[CapTP]($link.page('concepts')) bytes to a peer that can open a socket.
The session's keys stay at the two ends
([ADR-0086](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0086-byte-transparent-relay.md)).

# The relay only moves bytes

The CapTP handshake (`op:start-session`) and every message after it run
between the browser and the far peer. The relay never parses a frame and never
holds a capability. On a cleartext path it can still read every byte, so use
`noise-relay` when the relay must see only ciphertext. The Zig provider, the
other language providers, and the browser connector all support `noise-relay`.

# Reaching the other side

A browser has no socket to dial with, so the relay reaches the far side one of
two ways.

## Dialing a far peer

The browser names a target and the relay dials it. The Deno reference relay reads
the target off the WebSocket's query: `?to=host:port` opens a TCP socket, and
`?locator=<ocapn-uri>` opens an iroh channel. The relay then pipes the
WebSocket to whatever it dialed. Rooms runs the same forwarding behind its own `/relay` route.

[The browser names the peer it wants. The relay dials it over tcp or iroh and pipes the two together. The keyed session runs end to end, and the relay only moves bytes.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-relay-dial.svg'))

The app that hosts the relay sets the dial policy. [Rooms]($link.page('rooms'))
pins its TCP target to the room server, so a TCP dial reaches the room
and nothing else. An iroh target may be any node that proves its key, so the app caps
connections itself.

## When both ends are browsers

Neither browser can be dialed, so one registers its key with
the relay and leaves an inbound socket held there. A second browser, holding an
introduction that names that key, dials it, and the relay bridges the dialer to
the held socket. The room that made the introduction carries no part of the
session
([ADR-0088](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0088-fully-functioning-browser-peer.md)).

[One browser registers and waits. The other redeems an introduction by dialing that identity. The relay bridges the held socket to the dialer, and the keyed session runs end to end between the two browsers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-relay-rendezvous.svg'))

Both paths use the same forwarding code, so they share backpressure (a slow reader slows the sender) and shutdown behavior.

# Bounded resources

Backpressure on both legs caps the bytes in flight: when a reader stalls, the relay stops reading from its partner, so the stall reaches the sender. An idle timeout
reclaims silent sessions. A relayed connection costs buffers but no CapTP
session state. The hosting app sets its own limits on live sessions and held registrations, as [Rooms]($link.page('rooms')) does. The relay
counts bytes and sessions, never messages.

# The same contract on every runtime

`@glowcap/relay` is the Deno reference implementation. Python, Rust, and Racket forward,
apply backpressure, and reclaim idle sessions the same way.

# Locators

A `keyed-relay` locator names the waiting peer's key, and the dialing host
reaches it through its configured relay URL
([ADR-0088](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0088-fully-functioning-browser-peer.md)).
`noise-relay` uses the same path and seals the CapTP stream before it begins.
`noise-tcp`, `keyed-tcp`, `webrtc`, and `inproc` peers never use the relay,
and an `iroh` peer meets it only when a browser dials through it.
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) lists them all, and
the [Noise guide]($link.page('transports/noise')) covers the sealed path.

# Dial

Choose `noise-relay` for an end-to-end sealed stream. Use `keyed-relay` only
when the relay and network are already trusted. Each tab stops once the
runtime and locator are ready, and the binding page covers connect, fetch, and
call.

The snippets use `wss://` and trust the operating system's certificate store:
Zig passes `.system`, and the
bindings use the host WebSocket. The Zig provider also accepts cleartext `ws`
on a network you already trust. Its tests use a local certificate authority,
so none exercises a relay with a public certificate.

